tating that the value is the median."},{"n":28,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Two bowlers'' scores over 5 games are:\n\nAli: 40, 44, 46, 50, 60\nBen: 30, 42, 48, 52, 68\n\n(a) Calculate the mean score for each bowler.\n(b) Calculate the deviation of each of Ben''s scores from his mean.\n(c) Calculate the population standard deviation of Ben''s scores.\n(d) State which bowler is more consistent and justify your answer.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two bowlers'' scores over 5 games are:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ali: 40, 44, 46, 50, 60\nBen: 30, 42, 48, 52, 68"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the mean score for each bowler.\n(b) Calculate the deviation of each of Ben''s scores from his mean.\n(c) Calculate the population standard deviation of Ben''s scores.\n(d) State which bowler is more consistent and justify your answer."}]}]},"e":"Both means are 48. Ben: deviations −18, −6, 0, 4, 20 give 324 + 36 + 0 + 16 + 400 = 776; variance 155.2; σ ≈ 12.5. Ali: 64 + 16 + 4 + 4 + 144 = 232; variance 46.4; σ ≈ 6.8. Ali is more consistent despite the same mean.","m":9,"df":"hard","tp":"standard deviation","st":"standard deviation","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":28,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Populations σ = √(Σ(x − x̄)² / n). A larger σ means more variability about the mean.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate Ali''s mean score.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Add the five scores and divide by 5.","40 + 44 + 46 + 50 + 60."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":48,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate Ben''s mean score.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The mean is the total of the scores divided by how many there are.","Ben''s scores total 240 over five games."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":48,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the sum of the squared deviations for Ben, Σ(x − x̄)².","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Subtract 48 from each score, square it, then add.","(−18)² + (−6)² + 0² + 4² + 20²."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":776,"tolerance":0},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Calculate Ben''s population standard deviation, to 3 significant figures.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Variance = Σ(x − x̄)² ÷ n.","Divide 776 by 5, then take the square root."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":12.5,"tolerance":0.05},{"id":"e","label":"(e)","prompt":"State which bowler is more consistent and justify your answer.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compare the standard deviations of Ali and Ben.","A larger standard deviation means more spread."],"strand":"C(iv)","accepted":["ali, because his standard deviation is about 6.8 against ben''s 12.5, so his scores are closer to the mean"],"keywords":[["standard"],["deviation"],["against"],["ben''s"],["scores"],["closer"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 48. (b) 1 mark for 48. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the deviations, 1 for 776. (d) 3 marks: 1 for dividing by n, 1 for the variance 155.2, 1 for 12.5 (accept 12.4–12.6). (e) 2 marks: 1 for naming Ali, 1 for comparing the two standard deviations.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 48. (b) 1 mark for 48. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the deviations, 1 for 776. (d) 3 marks: 1 for dividing by n, 1 for the variance 155.2, 1 for 12.5 (accept 12.4–12.6). (e) 2 marks: 1 for naming Ali, 1 for comparing the two standard deviations.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","statistics"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Show the deviations in (c) — the squares are where method marks lie. In (e) compare the two σ values."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 48. (b) 1 mark for 48. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the deviations, 1 for 776. (d) 3 marks: 1 for dividing by n, 1 for the variance 155.2, 1 for 12.5 (accept 12.4–12.6). (e) 2 marks: 1 for naming Ali, 1 for comparing the two standard deviations."},{"n":29,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A school compares the journey times (minutes) of students and teachers.\n\nStudents (30 students): range 42, IQR 14, median 22\nTeachers (24 teachers): range 55, IQR 9, median 31\n\n(a) State which group has the more variable middle 50% and justify it.\n(b) A governor claims the students have more variable journeys because their range is smaller. Evaluate this claim.\n(c) Using the medians, describe the difference between the two groups.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A school compares the journey times (minutes) of students and teachers."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Students (30 students): range 42, IQR 14, median 22\nTeachers (24 teachers): range 55, IQR 9, median 31"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State which group has the more variable middle 50% and justify it.\n(b) A governor claims the students have more variable journeys because their range is smaller. Evaluate this claim.\n(c) Using the medians, describe the difference between the two groups."}]}]},"e":"IQR is the spread of the middle half: students 14 > teachers 9. But the teachers'' range of 55 exceeds the students'' 42, so the extremes belong to the teachers. Teachers'' median journey is 9 minutes longer.","m":6,"df":"hard","tp":"comparing data sets","st":"comparing data sets","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":29,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Compare centre and spread separately, and remember the range uses only the two extremes while the IQR uses the middle half.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State which group is more variable in the middle 50%, and justify it.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["The IQR measures the middle half of the data.","14 against 9."],"strand":"D(i)","accepted":["students, because their IQR of 14 is larger than the teachers'' 9, so their middle 50% covers a wider range"],"keywords":[["students"],["larger"],["teachers''"],["middle"],["covers"],["wider"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Evaluate the governor''s claim about the ranges.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compare 42 with 55: which range is larger?","The range is sensitive to just two values, the extremes."],"strand":"D(ii)","accepted":["the claim is wrong: the teachers'' range is 55 against the students'' 42, so the teachers'' journeys are more spread at the extremes; the students are only more variable in the middle"],"keywords":[["claim"],["wrong"],["teachers''"],["range"],["against"],["students''"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Describe the difference between the groups using the medians.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compare 31 with 22.","State the difference in minutes and what it means."],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["a typical teacher travels about 9 minutes longer than a typical student"],"keywords":[["typical"],["teacher"],["travels"],["minutes"],["longer"],["student"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for naming students, 1 for citing the IQRs. (b) 2 marks: 1 for correcting the range comparison, 1 for explaining the range''s sensitivity. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the 9-minute difference, 1 for saying what it means.","marking_criteria":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for naming students, 1 for citing the IQRs. (b) 2 marks: 1 for correcting the range comparison, 1 for explaining the range''s sensitivity. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the 9-minute difference, 1 for saying what it means.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Use the specific numbers in each answer; a comparison without figures earns no marks."},"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for naming students, 1 for citing the IQRs. (b) 2 marks: 1 for correcting the range comparison, 1 for explaining the range''s sensitivity. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the 9-minute difference, 1 for saying what it means."},{"n":30,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A company advertises ''average salaries of $48 000'' for 100 employees. The grouped salary data are:\n\n$10 000–20 000: 42 employees\n$20 000–30 000: 26\n$30 000–40 000: 14\n$40 000–60 000: 12\n$60 000–100 000: 6\n\n(a) Calculate the estimated mean using midpoints.\n(b) Explain why the advertised figure may be misleading and suggest a better measure.\n(c) Describe the distribution''s shape from the table.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A company advertises ''average salaries of "},{"type":"text","text":"$48 000'' for 100 employees. The grouped salary data are:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$10 000–20 000: 42 employees\n$"},{"type":"text","text":"20 000–30 000: 26\n"},{"type":"text","text":"$30 000–40 000: 14\n$"},{"type":"text","text":"40 000–60 000: 12\n"},{"type":"text","text":"$60 000–100 000: 6"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the estimated mean using midpoints.\n(b) Explain why the advertised figure may be misleading and suggest a better measure.\n(c) Describe the distribution''s shape from the table."}]}]},"e":"Σf x = $2 850 000 over 100 employees gives an estimated mean of $28 500. Since 82% earn under $30 000, the advertised $48 000 misrepresents a typical salary; the median is more representative. The long right tail makes the distribution positively skewed.","m":9,"df":"hard","tp":"histograms","st":"histograms","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":30,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Compare the estimated mean with the advertised claim and ask what a ''typical'' value means when the data are skewed.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the sum of f × midpoint, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Use midpoints 15 000, 25 000, 35 000, 50 000 and 80 000.","Multiply each by its frequency and add the five products."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":2850000,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the estimated mean salary, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The estimated mean is Σ(f × midpoint) divided by the total frequency.","Divide your total by the 100 employees."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":28500,"tolerance":0,"unit":"$"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Explain why the advertised figure is misleading and state a better measure.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["How many employees earn less than $30 000?","Which measure is resistant to a few very high salaries?"],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["82 of the 100 employees earn less than $30 000, so $48 000 is not typical; the median is a fairer measure because it is not raised by the few high earners"],"keywords":[["employees"],["earn"],["typical"],["median"],["fairer"],["measure"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Describe the shape of the distribution, using the table as evidence.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Where is the bulk of the employees?","Is there a long tail at the low or the high end?"],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["positively skewed: most employees are in the lower classes with a small tail of very high salaries"],"keywords":[["positively"],["skewed"],["employees"],["lower"],["classes"],["small"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the midpoints, 1 for $2 850 000. (b) 2 marks: 1 for dividing by 100, 1 for $28 500. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the comparison, 1 for citing the 82 employees, 1 for recommending the median. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''positively skewed'', 1 for the evidence.","marking_criteria":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the midpoints, 1 for $2 850 000. (b) 2 marks: 1 for dividing by 100, 1 for $28 500. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the comparison, 1 for citing the 82 employees, 1 for recommending the median. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''positively skewed'', 1 for the evidence.","response_tools":["statistics","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"statistics","response_guidance":"Draw the histogram in the workspace (widths differ, so densities matter) and use it as evidence in (d).","statistics_chart":"histogram"},"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the midpoints, 1 for $2 850 000. (b) 2 marks: 1 for dividing by 100, 1 for $28 500. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the comparison, 1 for citing the 82 employees, 1 for recommending the median. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''positively skewed'', 1 for the evidence."},{"n":31,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A water tank is filled and then used. Its volume (litres) is recorded hourly for 8 hours:\n\n0 h: 0, 1 h: 120, 2 h: 240, 3 h: 300, 4 h: 300, 5 h: 240, 6 h: 160, 7 h: 80, 8 h: 20\n\n(a) State the interval during which the tank is being filled.\n(b) Calculate the average rate of filling, in litres per hour, during that interval.\n(c) Calculate the average rate of use over the last 5 hours.\n(d) Explain what the horizontal section of the graph represents.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A water tank is filled and then used. Its volume (litres) is recorded hourly for 8 hours:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"0 h: 0, 1 h: 120, 2 h: 240, 3 h: 300, 4 h: 300, 5 h: 240, 6 h: 160, 7 h: 80, 8 h: 20"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the interval during which the tank is being filled.\n(b) Calculate the average rate of filling, in litres per hour, during that interval.\n(c) Calculate the average rate of use over the last 5 hours.\n(d) Explain what the horizontal section of the graph represents."}]}]},"e":"Filling runs 0→3 h reaching 300 litres: 100 litres/hour. The plateau 3→4 h shows no change. From 4 h to 8 h the volume falls from 300 to 20 litres, so the average use is 280 ÷ 5 = 56 litres/hour.","m":7,"df":"hard","tp":"line graphs","st":"line graphs","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":31,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"On a line graph, the gradient is a rate. A horizontal section means the quantity is constant.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the number of hours the tank is being filled.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Find where the volume stops increasing.","It reaches 300 litres and stays there."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":3,"tolerance":0,"unit":"hours"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the average rate of filling, in litres per hour.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Use the change in volume divided by the time taken.","300 litres in 3 hours."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":100,"tolerance":0,"unit":"litres/hour"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the average rate of use over the last 5 hours, in litres per hour.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The volume falls from 300 to 20 litres between 3 h and 8 h.","Divide the 280-litre decrease by 5 hours."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":56,"tolerance":0,"unit":"litres/hour"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain what the horizontal section represents.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["A horizontal line means the volume is not changing.","What would cause no change in volume?"],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["the volume is constant between 3 and 4 hours, so no water is added or used during that hour"],"keywords":[["volume"],["constant"],["hours"],["water"],["added"],["used"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the change and time, 1 for 100. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 280 litres, 1 for 56. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''constant volume'', 1 for the reason.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the change and time, 1 for 100. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 280 litres, 1 for 56. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''constant volume'', 1 for the reason.","response_tools":["graph","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"graph","response_guidance":"Plot the points in the graph workspace and join them, then read the gradients for (b) and (c)."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the change and time, 1 for 100. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 280 litres, 1 for 56. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''constant volume'', 1 for the reason."},{"n":32,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Two classes'' test scores (out of 50) are:\n\nClass 7A: 12, 18, 21, 23, 25, 28, 31, 34, 38, 42, 47\nClass 7B: 15, 17, 19, 20, 22, 26, 29, 33, 35, 39, 44\n\n(a) State the median of Class 7A.\n(b) State the median of Class 7B.\n(c) Explain how a back-to-back stem-and-leaf diagram helps compare the two classes more fairly than the two lists.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two classes'' test scores (out of 50) are:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Class 7A: 12, 18, 21, 23, 25, 28, 31, 34, 38, 42, 47\nClass 7B: 15, 17, 19, 20, 22, 26, 29, 33, 35, 39, 44"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the median of Class 7A.\n(b) State the median of Class 7B.\n(c) Explain how a back-to-back stem-and-leaf diagram helps compare the two classes more fairly than the two lists."}]}]},"e":"Both classes have 11 scores. 7A''s median is 28 and 7B''s is 26. The back-to-back diagram shares stems, so a reader can see that 7A has more high scores in the 30s and 40s while 7B is concentrated in the high teens and twenties.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"stem-and-leaf diagrams","st":"stem-and-leaf diagrams","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":32,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"A stem-and-leaf diagram keeps every value while sorting it; back-to-back versions share stems so comparisons are visual.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the median of Class 7A.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["There are 11 scores, so the median is the 6th.","Count in from the lowest."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":28,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State the median of Class 7B.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Order the 11 scores and take the 6th.","15, 17, 19, 20, 22, 26, ..."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":26,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Explain the advantage of a back-to-back stem-and-leaf diagram.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["What do both sides share in such a diagram?","Think about comparing spread and shape, not just the median."],"strand":"C(ii)","accepted":["both classes share the same stems, so their spread, shape and centre can be compared directly row by row"],"keywords":[["classes"],["share"],["stems"],["spread"],["shape"],["centre"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 28. (b) 1 mark for 26. (c) 2 marks: 1 for shared stems, 1 for the comparison benefit.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 28. (b) 1 mark for 26. (c) 2 marks: 1 for shared stems, 1 for the comparison benefit.","response_tools":["data_table","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"data_table","response_guidance":"Build the back-to-back table in the workspace (one stem column, a leaf column for each class)."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 28. (b) 1 mark for 26. (c) 2 marks: 1 for shared stems, 1 for the comparison benefit."},{"n":33,"t":"assess_parts","p":"The number of books read by 9 students in a month is:\n\n1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 6, 18\n\n(a) State the mode.\n(b) Calculate the mean.\n(c) State the median.\n(d) Explain which measure best represents a typical student here, and why the mean is misleading.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The number of books read by 9 students in a month is:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 6, 18"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the mode.\n(b) Calculate the mean.\n(c) State the median.\n(d) Explain which measure best represents a typical student here, and why the mean is misleading."}]}]},"e":"Mode 4, median 4, mean 44/9 ≈ 4.89. The single 18 is an outlier that lifts the mean above every value except 18 itself, so the median or mode describes a typical student better.","m":6,"df":"hard","tp":"mean, mode and median","st":"mean, mode and median","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":33,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"When one value is far from the rest, quote the median, and say why the mean is distorted.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the mode.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Which value appears most often?","Count the 4s."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":4,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the mean, to 2 decimal places.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Add all nine values.","Divide the total by 9."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":4.89,"tolerance":0.01},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"State the median.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["With nine values the median is the 5th.","One, two, two, three, four, ..."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":4,"tolerance":0},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"State which measure is most representative and explain why the mean misleads.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Which value is far from the others?","How does one extreme value affect a mean?"],"strand":"C(iv)","accepted":["the median (or mode) of 4, because the mean of 4.89 is raised by the single value of 18"],"keywords":[["median"],["mode"],["mean"],["raised"],["single"],["value"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the total 44, 1 for 4.89. (c) 1 mark. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the choice, 1 for the outlier argument.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the total 44, 1 for 4.89. (c) 1 mark. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the choice, 1 for the outlier argument.","response_tools":["statistics","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"statistics","response_guidance":"Enter the values in the statistics workspace and look at the dot plot: the outlier is visible there.","statistics_chart":"dot_plot"},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the total 44, 1 for 4.89. (c) 1 mark. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the choice, 1 for the outlier argument."},{"n":34,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A courier company measures delivery times (minutes) for 40 parcels:\n\n0 ≤ t < 10: 3\n10 ≤ t < 20: 9\n20 ≤ t < 30: 15\n30 ≤ t < 40: 8\n40 ≤ t < 60: 5\n\nAn advertisement promises delivery ''within 25 minutes''. (a) State how many parcels took less than 20 minutes.\n(b) Explain why you cannot read the number under 25 minutes directly from the table.\n(c) Estimate that number using the assumption of even spread within the 20 ≤ t < 30 class.\n(d) Evaluate the advertisement''s claim using your estimate.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A courier company measures delivery times (minutes) for 40 parcels:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"0 ≤ t < 10: 3\n10 ≤ t < 20: 9\n20 ≤ t < 30: 15\n30 ≤ t < 40: 8\n40 ≤ t < 60: 5"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"An advertisement promises delivery ''within 25 minutes''. (a) State how many parcels took less than 20 minutes.\n(b) Explain why you cannot read the number under 25 minutes directly from the table.\n(c) Estimate that number using the assumption of even spread within the 20 ≤ t < 30 class.\n(d) Evaluate the advertisement''s claim using your estimate."}]}]},"e":"Cumulative totals 3, 12, 27, 35, 40. At 20 minutes, 12 parcels. Assuming even spread, 12 + (5/10)(15) = 19.5 ≈ 20 parcels took under 25 minutes — half the total — so the advert overstates the service.","m":7,"df":"hard","tp":"cumulative frequency","st":"cumulative frequency","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":34,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Cumulative frequency interpolates inside a class: multiply the class frequency by the fraction of the class you have passed.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the cumulative frequency at 20 minutes.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Cumulative frequency at 20 minutes is the total of the classes below 20.","Add the frequencies 3 and 9."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":12,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Explain why the count for 25 minutes cannot be read directly.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Which class contains 25 minutes?","The table only gives whole-class totals."],"strand":"D(ii)","accepted":["25 lies inside the class 20 ≤ t < 30, and the table only gives the total for the whole class"],"keywords":[["lies"],["inside"],["class"],["table"],["gives"],["total"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Estimate how many parcels took less than 25 minutes, assuming an even spread in that class.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Half of the 20 ≤ t < 30 class lies below 25 minutes.","Add half of 15 to the 12 you already have."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":19.5,"tolerance":0.6},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Evaluate the advertisement''s claim.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["What percentage of 40 is your estimate?","Does ''within 25 minutes'' describe most deliveries?"],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["the claim only holds for about half the deliveries, so it is misleading as a general promise"],"keywords":[["claim"],["holds"],["half"],["deliveries"],["misleading"],["general"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 3 marks: 1 for identifying the class, 1 for the half-class fraction, 1 for 19.5–20. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the percentage, 1 for a fair evaluation.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 3 marks: 1 for identifying the class, 1 for the half-class fraction, 1 for 19.5–20. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the percentage, 1 for a fair evaluation.","response_tools":["statistics","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"statistics","response_guidance":"Draw the cumulative curve in the workspace and read across at 25 minutes to check your estimate.","statistics_chart":"cumulative_frequency"},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 3 marks: 1 for identifying the class, 1 for the half-class fraction, 1 for 19.5–20. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the percentage, 1 for a fair evaluation."},{"n":35,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A supermarket records, for 200 customers: the amount spent (£), whether they used a loyalty card, the number of items bought, and the time spent in the store (minutes).\n\n(a) State which variable is qualitative.\n(b) State which variable is discrete.\n(c) Explain why a histogram is suitable for ''amount spent'' but not for ''loyalty card''.\n(d) Explain one reason the recorded time spent in the store might be unreliable.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A supermarket records, for 200 customers: the amount spent (£), whether they used a loyalty card, the number of items bought, and the time spent in the store (minutes)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State which variable is qualitative.\n(b) State which variable is discrete.\n(c) Explain why a histogram is suitable for ''amount spent'' but not for ''loyalty card''.\n(d) Explain one reason the recorded time spent in the store might be unreliable."}]}]},"e":"Loyalty card is qualitative, items is discrete, amount spent and time are continuous. A histogram requires numerical classes, which a yes/no variable does not have. Time in store is a measured quantity and is therefore subject to recording error and to customers re-entering.","m":6,"df":"hard","tp":"classification of data","st":"classification of data","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":35,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Match the representation to the data type, and treat measured variables as estimates with error.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Name the qualitative variable.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Which variable is a label rather than an amount?","One of the four has only two possible responses."],"strand":"D(i)","accepted":["loyalty card"]},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Name the discrete variable.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Which variable is counted in whole numbers?","You cannot buy half an item in this record."],"strand":"D(i)","accepted":["number of items bought"],"keywords":[["number"],["items"],["bought"]],"minKeywordGroups":3},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Explain why a histogram suits ''amount spent'' but not ''loyalty card''.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["A histogram needs numerical classes.","How many categories does the loyalty card variable have?"],"strand":"D(ii)","accepted":["amount spent is continuous so it can be grouped into numerical classes, while the loyalty card has only two categories, which a bar chart shows"],"keywords":[["amount"],["spent"],["continuous"],["grouped"],["numerical"],["classes"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Give one reason the recorded time in the store may be unreliable.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["How exactly is a customer''s time measured?","Could a customer enter, leave and return?"],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["the entry and exit times may not be recorded accurately, or a customer may leave and return so the time is not a single continuous visit"],"keywords":[["entry"],["exit"],["times"],["recorded"],["accurately"],["customer"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''needs numerical classes'', 1 for the two-category point. (d) 2 marks: 1 for a concrete cause, 1 for the consequence.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''needs numerical classes'', 1 for the two-category point. (d) 2 marks: 1 for a concrete cause, 1 for the consequence.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Give a concrete reason in (d) — ''human error'' alone is too vague to earn the mark."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''needs numerical classes'', 1 for the two-category point. (d) 2 marks: 1 for a concrete cause, 1 for the consequence."},{"n":36,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Two groups of runners record their 5 km times (minutes).\n\nGroup A: 18, 20, 21, 22, 24, 25, 30\nGroup B: 19, 23, 23, 24, 25, 26, 27\n\n(a) State the median of each group.\n(b) State Q1 and Q3 for Group A.\n(c) Calculate the IQR of Group B.\n(d) Explain which group is more consistent and justify it with your values.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two groups of runners record their 5 km times (minutes)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Group A: 18, 20, 21, 22, 24, 25, 30\nGroup B: 19, 23, 23, 24, 25, 26, 27"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the median of each group.\n(b) State Q1 and Q3 for Group A.\n(c) Calculate the IQR of Group B.\n(d) Explain which group is more consistent and justify it with your values."}]}]},"e":"Medians 22 and 24. Group A: Q1 = 20, Q3 = 25, IQR = 5. Group B: Q1 = 23, Q3 = 26, IQR = 3. Group B is more consistent by both IQR and range.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"five-number summary and box plots","st":"five-number summary and box plots","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":36,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Consistency is a statement about spread: compare IQR and range, not the medians.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the median of Group A.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["With seven values the median is the 4th value in order.","Count in from the lowest: 18, 20, 21, 22."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":22,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State the median of Group B.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["With seven values the median is the 4th value in order.","Count in from the lowest: 19, 23, 23, 24."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":24,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"State Q1 of Group A.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Exclude the median 22 and take the lower three values.","The middle of 18, 20, 21."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":20,"tolerance":0},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"State Q3 of Group A.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The upper quartile is the median of the upper half.","The upper three values are 24, 25 and 30."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":25,"tolerance":0},{"id":"e","label":"(e)","prompt":"Calculate the IQR of Group B.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Q1 of B is 23 and Q3 is 26.","IQR = Q3 − Q1."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":3,"tolerance":0},{"id":"f","label":"(f)","prompt":"State which group is more consistent and justify it.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compare the two IQRs.","Also compare the two ranges."],"strand":"C(iv)","accepted":["group b, because its IQR of 3 is smaller than group a''s 5 and its range of 8 is smaller than 12","group b is more consistent: IQR 3 against 5"],"keywords":[[["standard deviation","spread","variation","variability","consisten"],["smaller","larger","closer","more","less","compare","mean"]],[["converge","converges","ratio","r","less than 1","|r|"],["shrink","smaller","finite","tends","approaches","sum"]]],"minKeywordGroups":1}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 1 mark. (d) 1 mark. (e) 2 marks: 1 for Q1 and Q3, 1 for 3. (f) 2 marks: 1 for naming Group B, 1 for citing both spreads.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 1 mark. (d) 1 mark. (e) 2 marks: 1 for Q1 and Q3, 1 for 3. (f) 2 marks: 1 for naming Group B, 1 for citing both spreads.","response_tools":["statistics","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"statistics","response_guidance":"Enter both data sets in the statistics workspace to draw comparable box plots, then answer the boxes.","statistics_chart":"box_plot"},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 1 mark. (d) 1 mark. (e) 2 marks: 1 for Q1 and Q3, 1 for 3. (f) 2 marks: 1 for naming Group B, 1 for citing both spreads."},{"n":37,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A shoe shop records the sizes sold in a day:\n\n3, 4, 4, 5, 5, 5, 5, 6, 6, 7, 8, 8, 9\n\n(a) State the mode and the median.\n(b) Calculate the mean.\n(c) Explain which measure should guide the shop''s ordering decision, and why.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A shoe shop records the sizes sold in a day:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"3, 4, 4, 5, 5, 5, 5, 6, 6, 7, 8, 8, 9"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the mode and the median.\n(b) Calculate the mean.\n(c) Explain which measure should guide the shop''s ordering decision, and why."}]}]},"e":"Mode 5, median 5, mean 75/13 ≈ 5.77. The mean is pulled up by the sizes 7, 8, 8 and 9, so for stocking decisions the mode is the right measure: it identifies the size with the highest demand.","m":7,"df":"hard","tp":"mean, mode and median","st":"mean, mode and median","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":37,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Choose the measure that fits the decision: the mode for the most common category, the median when there are outliers.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the mode.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The mode is the value that appears most often.","Count how many times each size occurs."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":5,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State the median.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["With thirteen values the median is the 7th in order.","Count in from the lowest size."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":5,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the mean, to 2 decimal places.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Add all thirteen sizes.","Divide by 13."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":5.77,"tolerance":0.02},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"State which measure should guide ordering and explain why.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["The shop needs the size most often requested.","Does the mean overstate demand for any size?"],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["the mode of 5, because it is the size most often sold and the shop should stock what customers actually ask for; the mean of 5.77 would over-order larger sizes"],"keywords":[["mode"],["size"],["often"],["sold"],["shop"],["stock"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the total 75, 1 for 5.77. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the choice, 1 for citing demand, 1 for explaining why the mean misleads.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the total 75, 1 for 5.77. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the choice, 1 for citing demand, 1 for explaining why the mean misleads.","response_tools":["statistics","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"statistics","response_guidance":"Draw the bar chart in the workspace to see the demand by size, then answer (d) with the figures.","statistics_chart":"bar_chart"},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the total 75, 1 for 5.77. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the choice, 1 for citing demand, 1 for explaining why the mean misleads."}]'::jsonb) as x
